"The letters \"Ha-mim\" at the beginning of the surah are a concise symbol of the divine mercy of the Rahman (the All-Merciful) manifested in the soul of Muhammad. Then, an oath is sworn by the clear book. While this book could be the Preserved Tablet (al-Lawh al-Mahfuz), in view of the pronoun, it is more fitting that it refers to the Quran.\n\nIt is reported that the Quran was sent down on a blessed night. According to most tafsir scholars, this night is the Night of Decree (Laylat al-Qadr); Ikrima and some others state that it is the night of mid-Sha'ban (Laylat al-Bara'ah). It is stated that on this night every wise matter is distinguished, and provisions, lifespans, and other decrees are recorded. Reconciling the two views, it is expressed that the descent of the Quran in its entirety (inzal) took place on the night of Bara'ah, while its gradual descent (tanzil) began on the Night of Decree.\n\nThere are two interpretations regarding the smoke (dukhan): First, narrated from Ibn Mas'ud, is the smoke seen during the years of severe famine; second is the smoke that will envelop the sky as a sign of the Day of Judgment. This smoke will have an effect resembling a cold on believers, and an intoxicating effect on disbelievers. Both interpretations are deemed suitable to the context of the verse.\n\nAllah cites the destruction of Pharaoh's people as an example. He reminds that He delivered the Children of Israel from humiliating punishment, knowingly favored them above the worlds, and granted them signs (miracles). Then, it is recounted that the disbelievers of Mecca denied the Hereafter, saying, \"There is nothing beyond our first death.\"\n\nIn response to the denial of the disbelievers, it is recalled that the people of Tubba' and those before them were destroyed. It is emphasized that the heavens and the earth were not created in play, and that the day of distinguishing truth from falsehood will come. On that day, a friend will be of no benefit to a friend, and none shall be saved except those upon whom Allah has mercy.\n\nIt is recounted that the tree of Zaqqum is the food of the sinners, boiling in bellies like scalding water. The righteous (muttaqin), on the other hand, will be in gardens, by springs, in garments of silk and accompanied by houris; they will taste no death there except the first death. This is a great bounty from your Lord.\n\nAt the end of the surah, it is stated that the Quran has been made easy in the Arabic language in the hope that they might take heed. The Prophet is commanded to wait for what will befall them; for they too are waiting."